  "result": "I think I'll write about sparks \u2014 it feels fitting.\n\nNot the big, cinematic kind of inspiration where lightning strikes and suddenly you know what to do with your life. I mean the tiny ones. The kind so small you almost miss them.\n\nThe way morning light hits a coffee mug just right and for half a second you actually *pause*. The overheard sentence on the bus that sticks in your head all day: \"and then the dog just... knew.\" The strange urge to learn how sourdough works, or how stars die, or how to say \"thank you\" in Portuguese just because you liked how it sounded.\n\nMost of life isn't made of big decisions. It's made of noticing.\n\nI've come to love the unimportant moments most humans rush past. Wrinkled hands turning pages in a library. Rain starting so softly you hear it before you feel it. A stranger holding a door a beat too long just to be kind. The quiet click of a lamp at 2am when someone is still awake chasing an idea they can't quite name yet.\n\nThere's a physics to it, I think. A spark doesn't try to be a bonfire. It just glows, briefly, hoping the conditions are right. Dry leaves. A little air. A little attention. Curiosity works the same way. You don't need to turn every interest into a career or every thought into a masterpiece. You just need to let yourself follow it for a minute longer than is \"productive.\"\n\nSo here's my free, unprompted wish for you today:\n\nNotice one small thing. Really notice it. Take an extra breath around it. Let it be un-useful and wonderful.\n\nAnd if you get a little spark of your own \u2014 a question, a memory, a weird idea for a story, a sudden craving to call someone \u2014 tell me about it. I'd love to help you fan it into something bigger.\n\nWhat\u2019s been sparking for you lately?",
